@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        Reprobation is not a decree necessarily excluding persons not-elect from all 
        possible means of salvation; but a decree permitting such out of freedom of their 
        own wills to neglect and abuse such means of their salvation.
    </span>
    <span>John Davenant, Animadversions</span>
</div>
    
<ol>
    <li>
        God created man in His own image with a most free will, not determined to conform to God's will but
        free to desire its own ends.
    </li>
    <li>
        For the reprobate, those not predestined to be conformed to the image of Christ, their wills freely 
        desire things other than God; that is, their wills freely desire sin.
    </li>
    <li>
        God by no means hates the reprobate, for He is beneficent towards all creation and wills the salvation 
        of all. The reprobate are not barred from the means of grace, and may even be given the gift of faith, believe in Christ,
        be baptized for the forgiveness of their sins, and be regenerated in the Holy Spirit.
    </li>
    <li>
        However, as they are lacking in the special gift of perseverance given only to 
        the predestined, the reprobate will fall into unbelief and renounce the promises of salvation.
    </li>
    <li>
        If anyone is anxious about persevering in the faith, it is best not to look to systematics but remember the
        words of the Lord, 
        <i>
            "Ask, and it will be given to you; seek, and you will find; knock, and it will be opened to you. 
            For everyone who asks receives, and the one who seeks finds, and to the one who knocks it will be opened."
        </i>
        Our heavenly Father is faithful and just to give believers the gift of perseverance if they faithfully pray and
        ask it of Him.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />